/tmp/claude-1000/-home-darrell-work-agents2/10650b69-2838-4be1-90f1-25413f54158e/scratchpad/budget2.
Attempt-3's specific fixes (bucket rows gated on .Configured, on-target
class text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400, `room <= 0` clamp, byte-identity
re-anchor) are all present and verified working as claimed. However the
oracle is not exhaustive, and a same-page contradiction survives outside
its coverage.

(1) Phantom-row reproduction (attempt-2 regression): re-ran
TestOracleW4BucketPresenceParity — living-only and healthcare-only cases
both correctly omit the unconfigured bucket's row on both surfaces. The
degenerate "both targets unconfigured but HasCombinedTarget true" case is
mathematically unreachable through the real metrics.Calculate: hasCombinedTarget
= (budgetTarget+healthcareTarget) > 0, hasBudgetTarget = budgetTarget > 0,
hasHealthcareTarget = healthcareTarget > 0 (internal/services/metrics/metrics.go:236,242,251).
If both individual targets are <= 0, their sum is <= 0, contradicting
hasCombinedTarget = true. No probe needed beyond the algebra; confirmed no
code path bypasses this invariant (only caller of Calculate's
budget/healthcare targets is metrics.BudgetTargets). Not a defect.

(2) SAME-PAGE SWEEP — FOUND A REAL CONTRADICTION, independent of the one
ruling 2026-08-29a already fixed:
  web/templates/components/kpis.html:129-142 — the Budget KPI card's own
  CONTAINER TINT (border/background: rose "over" vs emerald "healthy" vs
  neutral) is still computed from a bare-sign, un-dead-banded comparison:
      {{$budgetOver := and .Metrics.HasCombinedTarget (gt .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta 0.0)}}
      {{$budgetHealthy := and .Metrics.HasCombinedTarget (le .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta 0.0)}}
  while the card's HEADLINE text three lines below it independently
  re-implements the $1 dead-band with hardcoded literals instead of
  consuming .BudgetVerdict.IsOver/.IsUnder/.Health:
      {{if gt .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta 1.0}} ... over
      {{else if lt .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta -1.0}} ... under
      {{else}} On budget {{end}}
  Reproduced with a planted test (zzMetrics: CombinedCumulativeDelta=0.60,
  HasCombinedTarget=true, both buckets configured — the exact class of
  fixture the accept.sh oracle already uses elsewhere): the rendered card
  is
      <div class="... border-rose-200 dark:border-rose-800 bg-rose-50 dark:bg-rose-900/20" ...>
        ...>Budget</p>
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">On budget</p>
        ...Living: $2,000.60 of $2,000.00 ... $0.60 on target
        ...Health: $300.00 of $300.00 ... $0.00 on target
  i.e. the card's chrome is an alarming ROSE/RED "over budget" tint while
  its own headline says "On budget" and both decomposition rows say "on
  target," for a delta (v.Delta=0.60) that BuildBudgetVerdict classifies
  as Health=green, IsOver=false, IsUnder=false. This is the SAME figure
  (CombinedCumulativeDelta == BudgetVerdict.Delta) rendered by TWO
  independent classifiers on the SAME card: the tint keeps the pre-W4
  bare-sign rule the ruling condemned, the headline keeps its own
  hardcoded copy of onBudgetEps=1.0 instead of reading .BudgetVerdict.
  Ruling 2026-08-29a's own words: "a threshold applied to a figure must
  live in ONE classification source consumed by every surface that
  renders the same figure." The card tint is a surface rendering that
  figure and is not consuming that source. This is not "distinctly
  labeled" — it's one card, one figure, contradicting itself: a
  danger-colored container wrapping "on target" text. Attempt 3's fix
  list (per .swarm/W-RUN-SPEC.md Attempt-3 block) only mentions bucket
  rows, on-target class, room clamp, and byte-identity anchor — it never
  touched this pre-existing tint logic, so the defect ruling 29a exists to
  prevent is still reachable, just moved from the per-bucket rows to the
  card's own container color vs its own headline/rows.
  Reachable range: any 0 < CombinedCumulativeDelta <= $1.00 (tint=rose,
  headline=on budget/neutral) or -$1.00 <= delta < 0 (tint=emerald,
  headline=on budget — milder, both read as "fine," not flagged as a
  contradiction here).
  Not caught by .swarm/tier3/W4/accept.sh because that oracle only
  greps rendered TEXT (row presence, status words, $-0.00, contrast
  classes on the decomposition rows) — it never inspects the outer card's
  border/bg classes.

  Headline vs decomposition (two on-target buckets summing past the
  headline's own dead-band, e.g. living=-0.99, healthcare=-0.99, total
  =-1.98): investigated and NOT a defect. IsUnder is computed from the
  TOTAL v.Delta, decomposition Status is computed per-bucket from
  independent bucket deltas — these are two different figures, not the
  same figure rendered twice, so ruling 29a's "same figure, one source"
  requirement does not apply between them, and the verdict sentence
  ("$1.98 room") stays numerically consistent with the headline either
  way. Distinctly labeled, not a violation.

(3) -0.0 hunt: formatMoney (internal/templates/render.go:425) does NOT
  self-guard against negative zero — `negative := v < 0` is false for
  IEEE754 -0.0, so an un-abs()'d -0.0 passed to formatMoney prints
  "-0.00" (verified with a standalone repro: math.Copysign(0,-1) prints
  "-0.00" via the same "%.2f" pattern formatMoney uses). The W4 sentence
  path is now safe only because classifyVerdictSentence clamps `room` to
  literal 0 when <= 0, and every Delta/OverAmount rendered through the
  templates on this page is wrapped in the `abs` template func
  (verified: `abs .BudgetVerdict.Living.Delta`, `abs
  .BudgetVerdict.Healthcare.Delta`, `abs .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta`
  x2, `abs .Metrics.PerMonthDelta`, `abs .Metrics.HealthcarePerMonthDelta`
  — grepped every formatMoney call site in kpis.html and
  dashboard-verdict-bar.html). No unguarded -0.0 escape found on this
  page today. This is fragile (relies on every call site remembering
  `abs`, not on formatMoney itself being safe) but not currently
  reachable, so not failing on it — flagging as residual risk only.

(4) Refund-heavy window (large negative actuals -> "spend up to $X"
